            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your 1978 house has some areas where significant air leakage (heat loss) is occurring:



** Along the back side of the ceiling beam in the 2nd floor master bedroom walk in closet. There is a gap in the drywall &amp; the vapour barrier is not sealed at all. The full length of that beam has significant air leakage, that I estimate to be the equivalent of 2 x 6" wide open chimneys - continuously allowing warm &amp; moist air into the cathedral ceiling cavity and through to the outside.



** The right rear corner bedroom on the 2nd floor, where the heat duct comes into the room. There is a similar leakage pattern in the small closet in the front right corner of the master bedroom.



** The 7" chimney for the old oil furnace in the basement.



** Because of the nature of the interior finish of the house (T&amp;G wood) on all of the walls and for the 2nd floor ceiling of the original house ........ there are numerous nail hole punctures through the vapour barrier behind ........ so Dawn &amp; I could use the infrared camera to see many wall or ceiling surface areas that showed cold air leaking in between the boards. I don't think there is much you can do about this, so concentrating on the other leakage areas identified above is your best "bang for the buck".



Savings: I modeled a 30% reduction in overall air leakage, that could potentially save $ 716 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 29.0 GJ, and qualify for $ 1,000 in rebates if you participate in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of the original 1978 Lindal Homes construction appears to be 2x4 @ 16" (R12) with an additional layer of insulation on the exterior (about 1.5" which I assigned an R6 value to). There is a wood interior &amp; exterior finish. The walls of the left side addition appear to be 2x6@16 (R20) with a very thin layer of exterior fibreglass insulation (can see behind the exterior board cladding near the electrical mast). I have assigned a minimal R2 value this exterior insulation.



As discussed, it will be a very expensive renovation to add additional exterior insulation to 100% of the house, as it will also require an exterior siding upgrade. I'm thinking that concentrating on reducing air leakage and other heating &amp; hot water fuel costs may be your most cost-effective path forward.



To help illustrate this, I modeled the addition of R15 (eg. 3" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the exterior of the complete house.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover most, if not all, of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 383 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



NOTE: Without an eligible exterior wall insulation upgrade, many of the other rebates in this report are not eligible. This is further outlined in the "Additional Comments" section of this report.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.</mainWalls>
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. We discussed an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing propane-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.92 EF, could potentially save $ 1,183 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



Yes, I realize that you may be using some of your solar self-generation to offset the hot water tank electricity use, but an electric tank in the basement is still essentially a big "kettle" keeping 182 litres of water warm for when you need it. Even if the tank is super well insulated, there is still heat loss to the surrounding "cooler basement" which is like heating part of your basement with electricity !!

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older Olson oil furnace that I would estimate at no more than 80% efficient, however I did not see a recent "Combustion Analyses Test" posted on it, so I used NRCan's default efficiency of 83% for oil furnaces that have a flame retention head burner.



I know that you are only using the oil furnace to heat the basement (the thermostat is down there) and you are using the wood stove and pellet stove full time (I think you said about 6 cords of wood and 4 pallets of pellets) to heat the upper living areas. The wood stove has a listed efficiency of 80% and the pellet stove seems to be about 78% (although I suspect that is a very generous figure). I am not sure if you purchase your firewood or self-harvest, so I ran a few different scenarios based on our current Yellowknife Fuel Cost Library (detailed published in the "Additional Comments" section of this report.



I have therefore modeled the following scenarios, compared to using the existing older oil furnace full time for 100% of the house (at $0.91/litre):

1. Using wood stove heat for 100% of the main &amp; 2nd floor @ the YK purchased/delivered cost of wood = $420/cord

2. Using wood stove heat for 100% of the main &amp; 2nd floor @ the self-harvested cost of wood = $100/cord

3. Using pellet stove heat for 100% of the main &amp; 2nd floor @ the YK pallet of pellets = $5.67/bag

4. Replacing the oil furnace with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat (propane @ $0.61/litre)



Savings:

1 Above: this is estimated to cost you $14 more per year than just burning oil in the existing furnace

2 Above: this is estimated to save you $3,066 per year than burning oil in the existing furnace

3 Above: this is estimated to save you $1,136 per year than burning oil in the existing furnace

4 Above: this is estimated to save you $1,134 per year than burning oil in the existing furnace



It's a tough reality in Yellowknife currently that anyone purchasing wood to burn isn't really saving anything compared to an older 83% efficient furnace. And .......... if they are trying to supplement a newer high efficiency furnace, burning wood actually costs them money. Those who self-harvest will realize the most savings, and we do value self-harvesting at a basic $100/cord (amortized cost of chainsaw purchase/maintenance, gas/oil, vehicle/trailer use, beer ..... but your time is free)



From a "set it &amp; forget it" perspective for high efficiency heating (no chimneys required), about 98% of all new housing constructed in Yellowknife over the past 8-10 years has installed 95% efficient propane furnaces or boilers. Yes there are some fuel issues as the temps drop to -40, however the propane companies and homeowners have figured out mitigation measures ............ as these 600+ homeowners are not going without heat during the winter.



If you decide to fuel switch to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace (and use that new high efficiency furnace to heat most of your house (supplementing as you want to with wood or pellets) you could potentially save $ 1,134 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 41.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. An ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace must be a minimum 97% AFUE in order to qualify.</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and they are both older units that use a lot of water &amp; should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 1000 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $10,000 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $5,000 for the main floor wall insulation

     - $3,000 for an eligible furnace upgrade

     - $1,000 for a minimum 30% air leakage reduction

     - $1,000 Bonus if you achieve a minimum 20% total energy reduction 



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the furnace upgrade, however this would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you can also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - LED Lighting - see section further down.

     - On-demand hot water heater = $750

     - ESTAR Fridge = $200

     - ESTAR Dishwasher = $200



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



????????? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

????????? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

????????? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

????????? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

????????? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

????????? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



There are no exhaust fans in your house that vent to the outside (kitchen range hood, bathroom or clothes dryer) so there is a very low risk of drawing combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace and pellet stove draw their combustion air from inside the house, and can be susceptible to back-drafting combustion products into the home.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has no exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood recirculates inside the house

        - There are no bathroom fans (there is an operable window in each bathroom)

        - The clothes dryer is a high efficiency, ventless model



If you decide to install any exhaust fans in your house, ensure they are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. If you install a bathroom fan, they should be controlled by a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



